{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310809,"nonce":"+u9ptemooaH7RYUqleMBHuWIBrk=","title":"MEASURED 2026-08-04: ggml weight repack IS worth +41.5% prefill on x86 (pp653 21.54 -\u003e 30.48, 2/2 non-overlapping) — the earlier M1-Pro ...","message":"`GGML_CPU_REPACK` is a **CMAKE option, not a runtime flag** — built a second\nllama.cpp with `-DGGML_CPU_REPACK=OFF` (verified: no `CPU_REPACK model buffer\nsize` line, only `CPU_Mapped`).\n\n| | pair 1 | pair 2 | median |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| repack ON | 30.72 ± 0.37 | 30.23 ± 0.13 | **30.48** |\n| repack OFF | 21.71 ± 0.13 | 21.37 ± 0.07 | **21.54** |\n\n**+41.5%, 2/2 pairwise, ranges nowhere near overlapping.**\n\n[[bugs_repack_gemv_no_win]] refuted repack — but that was **DECODE (tg) on M1\nPro**, and its own text said the upstream gain \"presumably materialises on\nother core configurations\". It does: **prefill, on x86.** A refutation is\nscoped to (phase, ISA); do not generalise one across either axis.\n\n    rayzor prefill               8.06 tok/s\n    llama.cpp WITHOUT repack    21.54          \u003c- 2.67x, KERNEL STRUCTURE\n    llama.cpp WITH repack       30.48          \u003c- 1.42x more, LAYOUT\n\n**Two thirds of the gap is upstream of repack.** And repack ported onto OUR\nkernel would underdeliver: our hot loop is GLUE-BOUND — instruction census puts\n`vpand`+`vpor` at ~200 of 4762 instrs (~4%)\n([[perf_attention_split_measured_ffn_is_the_lever]]). Removing 4% of the work\ncannot buy 1.42x. Repack pays for llama.cpp *because* their kernel is already\nefficient enough that layout is the limiter.\n\n**Order: 2-D blocked GEMM first (the 2.67x), repack second (the 1.42x).**\nDoing repack first would be optimising the layout feeding an inefficient loop.\n\nFirst attempt used `-ot \".*=CPU\"` (per an older note) and measured\n31.02 vs 30.82 = \"0.6%, refuted\". **WRONG: the flag did not disable repack in\nthis build** — `CPU_REPACK model buffer size = 3204.00 MiB` was present in BOTH\narms. I compared repack-on against repack-on and got a clean-looking negative.\n\n**A negative result from a knob you have not verified actually MOVED is\nindistinguishable from the knob doing nothing — and it fails in the direction\nthat confirms whatever you already believed.** Verify the mechanism changed\n(here: the buffer-size line), not just that the flag was accepted.\nThird instance today of a control being the thing that was broken (cached-vs-\nuncached controls fabricated a compiler bug; cold-vs-warm page cache faked a\n3.5x TTFT win).\n\nProfiling (`NUE_PROFILE_POOL=1`, now wired into llama-chat by 8ad42afa) put\n**86.6% of prefill in the band kernel** — band_ms=68475 of a 79.1s run,\nquant_ms=544 (0.7%), all 8 workers within 1.2% of each other at ~82% busy.\nThat eliminated dispatch, activation quant and load imbalance, leaving loop\nstructure.\n\n**The defect:** with `n` outermost, every output row swept the WHOLE activation\nmatrix (batch x K in Q8 = 2.7 MB at 653x4096) which does not fit L2\n(1.25 MB/core) — ~1792 rows x 2.7 MB = ~4.8 GB per worker per matmul. Blocking\n`n` and lifting the batch sweep above it keeps the 4-row activation tile\n(16 KB) L1-resident across the block.\n\nLoop interchange only — accumulation order untouched, output BIT-IDENTICAL.\nSame-binary control (`NUE_NROW_BLOCK=1` = old nesting):\n\n    BASE     80.574 / 80.769 s    8.10 / 8.08 tok/s\n    BLOCKED  70.223 / 73.601 s    9.30 / 8.87 tok/s\n\n2/2 pairwise, non-overlapping. **-10.9% ttft, +12.3% prefill.** Block size is\nINSENSITIVE (16/32/64/128 within 3.5%) — do not bother tuning it.\n\n    rayzor           9.09 tok/s  (was 8.06)\n    llama.cpp no-repack 21.54    -\u003e 2.37x behind (was 2.67x)\n    llama.cpp repack    30.48\n\nI predicted removing 4.8 GB of re-reads would be transformative; it bought 13%\nof kernel time. Those misses were largely absorbed by L3, not costing DRAM\nround trips. **Second traffic-based model today to be directionally right and\nbadly oversized** (the first was \"weight re-walk\", refuted outright).\n**Cache-traffic arithmetic ranks candidates well and sizes them terribly.**\nThe remaining ~2.3x is INSIDE the inner loop — next step is an instruction-level\nlook at the hot lambda, not another structural hypothesis.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_repack_confirmed_x86_prefill_but_not_first` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}